@section('policy-content')
    <section>
        <h2>Who handles your information</h2>
        <p>St. John Nepomucene Parish uses this system to administer parish services and records. The parish office is the initial contact for privacy questions. Before adopting this draft, the parish must confirm the responsible legal entity and its designated privacy officer's contact details.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Information used</h2>
        <p>Depending on the service, records may include names, contact details, addresses, birth and family details, religious or sacramental information, submitted supporting documents, appointment and facility details, and payment or donation references. Security and activity records may also contain technical information such as IP addresses. Religious affiliation and some supporting documents contain sensitive personal information.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Why information is used</h2>
        <p>Information supports processing requests, checking requirements, scheduling services, communicating updates, maintaining parish and financial records, preventing misuse, and preparing reports. Before adoption, the parish must confirm and disclose the lawful basis for each purpose, including the applicable basis for sensitive personal information. Reading this notice or browsing the website is not consent to every use of your data.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Access and service providers</h2>
        <p>Authorized parish personnel access information according to their responsibilities. Hosting, storage, email delivery, and online payment providers may process information needed to provide their services. Online payments use an external payment service. Before adoption, the parish must confirm the providers, recipient categories, any overseas processing, and safeguards that apply to the deployed system.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Retention and protection</h2>
        <p>Canceling or allowing a request to expire does not automatically erase its record or payment history. The parish must approve retention periods for appointments, documents, sacramental records, financial records, logs, and backups before adopting this policy, and explain lawful exceptions. Access controls and private record links help protect information; keep your own access details secure.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Your privacy rights</h2>
        <p>Subject to applicable law and its exceptions, you may request access to and correction of your personal data, object to processing, or request erasure or blocking. Where applicable, you may withdraw consent and request data portability. The office may need to verify your identity or authority before releasing or changing records. Some records may need to be retained despite a request.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Children and representatives</h2>
        <p>Provide a child's information only as a parent, guardian, or properly authorized representative. Supply only what the requested service requires. Contact the office if you believe information was submitted without appropriate authority.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
        <h2>Complaints and review</h2>
        <p>You may raise a concern with the parish office and lodge a complaint with the National Privacy Commission. This draft needs parish and qualified legal or privacy review before adoption, including a confirmed retention schedule, privacy contact, and processing grounds.</p>
    </section>
    <p>Learn more from the <a href="https://privacy.gov.ph/data-subject-rights/">National Privacy Commission's guide to data subject rights</a> and the <a href="https://privacy.gov.ph/Data-Privacy-Act/">Data Privacy Act of 2012</a>.</p>
